--TEST--
`Core\Markdown::fromHtml` writes a table of plain cells as a GFM table, gives a code block with three backticks a fence of four, writes a `<br>` between text as a backslash, and writes a `dl` as HTML on one line — `rule:core-classes/markdown-from-html`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// covers: Core\Markdown::fromHtml
// A table with only plain cells and no colspan is written as a GFM table.
echo Core\Markdown::fromHtml("<table><thead><tr><th>Name</th><th>Price</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><b>Tea</b></td><td>3</td></tr><tr><td>Cake</td><td>5</td></tr></tbody></table>");
echo "--\n";
// The code has a run of three backticks, so the fence has four.
echo Core\Markdown::fromHtml("<pre><code class=\"language-md\">Use ```\nto start code.\n</code></pre>");
echo "--\n";
// A break between two pieces of text is a backslash at the end of the line.
echo Core\Markdown::fromHtml("<p>first line<br>second line</p>");
echo "--\n";
// A description list has no Markdown syntax, so it stays HTML on one line.
echo Core\Markdown::fromHtml("<dl><dt>Tea</dt><dd>hot</dd></dl>");
--EXPECT--
| Name | Price |
| --- | --- |
| **Tea** | 3 |
| Cake | 5 |
--
````md
Use ```
to start code.
````
--
first line\
second line
--
<dl><dt>Tea</dt><dd>hot</dd></dl>
